<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">charm</ansi> spell

The <ansi fg="command">Charm</ansi> spell reaches into a creature's mind and bends its will to
yours. It will follow you and fight at your side as your companion.

A stubborn mind fights you hardest. Raw strength is no defence at all,
so the biggest thing in the room can be the easiest to take, and by far
the worst to lose hold of. A creature already fighting resists more
strongly, and one already fighting you resists most of all.

<ansi fg="white-bold">The hold ends.</ansi> There is no warning, and no sign of it weakening. How
completely you won the contest decides how long you keep the creature,
so the same spell cast twice can give you very different amounts of
time. You are never told how much you have.

A creature caught asleep is easiest to take, and held longest.

When the hold ends, if you are standing beside it, it turns on you. If
you are elsewhere it simply goes back to being what it was. Either way
the conviction you spent holding it returns to you.

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">cast charm</ansi>             Attempts to charm your current combat target.
  <ansi fg="command">cast charm [target]</ansi>    Targets a specific creature by name.

<ansi fg="yellow">Casting:     </ansi> Long channel, requires deep concentration
<ansi fg="yellow">Target:      </ansi> Single creature
<ansi fg="yellow">Defense:     </ansi> Defy (the creature's willpower and its skill at
              arguing back)
<ansi fg="yellow">Duration:    </ansi> Decided by how completely you won
<ansi fg="yellow">Conv. Cost:  </ansi> Very high, and more is held for as long as the
              bond lasts
<ansi fg="yellow">School:      </ansi> Manifestation

<ansi fg="white-bold">Notes:</ansi>
  - Charm cannot be used on other players.
  - Merchants, powerful named creatures, and anyone who is not a
    fighter are beyond your reach.
  - A charmed creature keeps any gear you hand it, and grows stronger
    fighting at your side. Both of those cut two ways.
  - A powerful creature is no harder to charm than a weak one. It is
    only far worse to be standing next to when the hold ends.
  - Letting one go is not a peaceful parting. <ansi fg="command">dismiss</ansi> and it turns
    on you at once, so free a slot before you need one rather than in
    the middle of a fight.
  - You may only hold a limited number of companions at once.
  - Logging out destroys anything you have charmed, along with
    whatever it was carrying. Take back anything you lent it first.

<ansi fg="magenta-bold">See also:</ansi> <ansi fg="command">help manifestation</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help companion</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help dismiss</ansi>
